26:  {category:'Studying DNA',title:'Sequencing and genome analysis',text:'DNA sequencing determines the order of bases in a sample. Methods range from chain-termination sequencing to high-throughput short- and long-read technologies. Real workflows include sample preparation, quality checks, read alignment or assembly, and interpretation against references; errors, coverage gaps, contamination, and reference bias matter. A sequence by itself is not a complete explanation of phenotype, ancestry, health, or function.'},
27:  {category:'Studying DNA',title:'Genome editing and biotechnology',text:'CRISPR-associated systems use guide molecules and proteins to target nucleic-acid sequences; related tools can cut, regulate, or otherwise modify genetic material. Outcomes depend on the organism, delivery, target, repair processes, and verification. Genome editing is distinct from simply “rewriting a gene,” and off-target effects, mosaicism, ecological consequences, and ethical considerations require careful assessment. This Codex is informational, not an experimental protocol.'},
28:  {category:'Human genetics and society',title:'Human genetic variation',text:'Two human genomes are overwhelmingly similar, yet millions of common and rare variants exist across populations. Most individual traits reflect many genetic variants and environmental influences, and categories such as ancestry or population are complex and do not map neatly onto social race. Genetic association is not proof of causation. Responsible interpretation considers consent, privacy, uncertainty, representation, and potential harms.'},
